---
/**
 * The content map: what the landing page has to say, in order, and which
 * page owns everything it only points at. Each row names where the fact
 * comes from, so the page can be checked against the project.
 */
import Lab from "../../layouts/Lab.astro";

const landing = [
  {
    section: "Hero",
    says: "What Graft is and who it is for, the install command, four facts: MDX in Git, one Zod schema, MCP and a CLI, MIT and self-hosted.",
    proof: "The headline, with the agent marks, a Studio crop and the compile command in its lines",
    source: "README, opening",
  },
  {
    section: "Agents and stack",
    says: "Graft works with the agents, frameworks and hosts you already use, because it is made of MDX, a TypeScript schema, Git and Postgres.",
    proof: "Three moving rows of real marks",
    source: "README; the framework guides",
  },
  {
    section: "Built for agents from day one",
    says: "Agents were part of the design, not connected later. An agent can do everything an editor can, at no extra cost, under the same rules.",
    proof: "Three questions to ask any CMS, with Graft's answers",
    source: "README, Agents operate it",
  },
  {
    section: "No code needed",
    says: "Someone with no technical knowledge tells their agent what they want and gets a site they can edit.",
    proof: "The exchange the docs give an agent for this job",
    source: "Set up a site for someone",
  },
  {
    section: "The model",
    says: "Git is the source. `graft compile` checks every file and derives the index, the typed reads and the MCP tools. If Git and the index disagree, Git wins.",
    proof: "One diagram: your repo, the compile, three outputs",
    source: "How Graft works",
  },
  {
    section: "The page is the file",
    says: "Content is a file you can edit. The schema refuses a bad write and says how to fix it.",
    proof: "An editable file beside the page it renders, with the real validation error",
    source: "Schema; `graft compile --json`",
  },
  {
    section: "Typed reads",
    says: "The schema that checks a write types the read. No codegen. Nine frameworks.",
    proof: "The schema beside the read in Next.js, Astro and SvelteKit, and the list of SDKs",
    source: "Reading content; the framework guides",
  },
  {
    section: "Agents",
    says: "Agents work over MCP and the CLI, read the real schema, and every error carries a fix.",
    proof: "A replayed MCP exchange: refused, corrected, compiled",
    source: "Agents overview; MCP tools",
  },
  {
    section: "The human gate",
    says: "A destructive call never runs the first time. An approval works once, for one exact input, and the requester cannot decide it.",
    proof: "An approval the visitor approves or denies",
    source: "Audit and approvals",
  },
  {
    section: "Studio",
    says: "An optional editor for the rest of the team. It writes the same files, so nothing is locked inside it.",
    proof: "The real Studio, live, on this site's content",
    source: "Studio",
  },
  {
    section: "Everything in the box",
    says: "Search, previews, roles, history, images and safe content come with Graft.",
    proof: "A grid of small pictures of each one at work",
    source: "Search; Branching and previews; Studio hosting; Audit and approvals; Assets; MDX safety",
  },
  {
    section: "Storage engines",
    says: "No database to start: one SQLite file, deployed with your app. Postgres when users write data, and an error that tells you when.",
    proof: "The two engines side by side and the real NEEDS_DATABASE error",
    source: "Storage engines; README",
  },
  {
    section: "Owned primitives",
    says: "Comments, commerce, SEO and more arrive as TypeScript in your repo, not as a plugin.",
    proof: "The real output of `graft add comments`",
    source: "Owned primitives",
  },
  {
    section: "Questions",
    says: "Dashboard, open source, Git, who it is for, adding a feature.",
    proof: "Read from this site's own home document",
    source: "content/pages/home.mdx",
  },
  {
    section: "Close",
    says: "One command, no account, no database until you want one. Links to the guide and the docs.",
    proof: "The install command as a button",
    source: "Getting started",
  },
];

const pages = [
  {
    page: "Agents",
    path: "/agents",
    draft: "/lab/agents",
    state: "New. First draft built",
    owns: "Built for agents from day one, in full: what an agent can do, the tools it gets, how to connect one, and the approval before a delete.",
  },
  {
    page: "Studio",
    path: "/studio",
    draft: "/lab/studio",
    state: "New. First draft built",
    owns: "The editor for your content team: writing, drafts, review, publish, history and restore, hosting it for a team, and roles.",
  },
  {
    page: "Start",
    path: "/start",
    draft: "/lab/start",
    state: "New. First draft built",
    owns: "Getting a site with no technical knowledge: what to tell your agent, the three questions it asks, and what you end up with.",
  },
  {
    page: "Why Graft",
    path: "/why",
    draft: "/lab/why",
    state: "Redone in the new design",
    owns: "The argument the landing page only states: why a CMS built for agents differs from one that added them later, what to ask any CMS, and who Graft is not for.",
  },
  {
    page: "Security",
    path: "/security",
    draft: "/lab/security",
    state: "Redone in the new design",
    owns: "How approvals are enforced, the audit log, why content cannot run code, and how sign-in plugs into your own.",
  },
  {
    page: "Self-hosting",
    path: "/self-host",
    state: "Proposed",
    owns: "Running it yourself: with no database, on your own Postgres, or as its own server. Branches and previews, hardening, upgrades.",
  },
  {
    page: "Features",
    path: "/features",
    state: "Proposed",
    owns: "Everything in the box, one section each: search, images and files, roles, history, forms and orders, comments, commerce, SEO.",
  },
  {
    page: "Docs and changelog",
    path: "/docs",
    state: "Exist",
    owns: "Every how-to and reference: the nine framework guides, functions, auth, the CLI and SDK references, error codes, upgrading, and what shipped.",
  },
];
---

<Lab
  title="Graft landing content map"
  description="What the Graft landing page says, in order, and which page owns the rest."
  variant="map"
  theme="system"
>
  <section class="screen lab-map">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1 class="h2">What the landing page says, and what lives elsewhere</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The landing page states each thing once and proves it with the product. Anything that needs
        an argument or a reference belongs to another page, and the landing page links to it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="lab-map-title">The landing page, in order</h2>
      <div class="lab-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Landing page sections">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Section</th>
              <th scope="col">What it says</th>
              <th scope="col">How it proves it</th>
              <th scope="col">Where the facts come from</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              landing.map((row, i) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">
                    <span class="lab-table-n">{i + 1}</span> {row.section}
                  </th>
                  <td>{row.says}</td>
                  <td>{row.proof}</td>
                  <td>{row.source}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="lab-map-title">The other pages</h2>
      <div class="lab-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Other pages">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Page</th>
              <th scope="col">State</th>
              <th scope="col">What it owns</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              pages.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">
                    {row.page} <code>{row.path}</code>{"draft" in row && row.draft ? <a class="lab-table-link" href={row.draft}>Open the draft</a> : null}
                  </th>
                  <td>{row.state}</td>
                  <td>{row.owns}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Lab>
